.page explorer 1 the Gensokyo Explorer, the file manager of the desktop

# SYNOPSIS
*explorer* [_PATH_]
*explorer* *--selftest*
*explorer* *--help* | *--version*

# DESCRIPTION
`explorer` shows a folder as a list or as icons and lets you copy, move,
rename, delete and open what is in it. It is the "Gensokyo Explorer" of the
start menu, the folder button of the panel and the desktop icons for Home,
Netherworld and the root.

With no _PATH_ it opens your home folder. A relative _PATH_ is taken from the
current directory. If _PATH_ is a file, its folder is shown with the file
selected. A path that does not exist is reported in a dialog and the home
folder is shown instead.

Everything the Explorer does to files happens as a job that works in small
slices, so the window never stops answering, however big the folder or the
copy is. A job that takes more than a moment gets a progress window with a
Cancel button; jobs started meanwhile wait their turn. Problems do not stop a
job: they are collected and shown in one list when it is finished.

# THE WINDOW
@ Menu bar
    File, Edit, View, Go and Help. They open with the mouse or from the
    keyboard (Alt and the first letter, or F10; then the arrows, Enter and
    Esc).
@ Toolbar
    Back, Forward, Up and Refresh, the address field, the list and icon
    buttons, and New Folder. Type an address (a path, `~` for home) and press
    Enter to go there; Esc puts the old address back.
@ Places
    Home, the root, the Netherworld and the mounted volumes listed in
    `/proc/mounts`. A click goes there. Dropping files on a place moves or
    copies them there.
@ The folder
    In list mode the columns are Name, Size, Modified, Type, Permissions and
    Owner (the last ones disappear when the window is narrow). Clicking a
    heading sorts by it, clicking it again reverses the order. Folders always
    come first and numbers in names are compared as numbers (file2 before
    file10). Icon mode shows a grid of icons with their names.
@ Status bar
    The number of items, how many are hidden, what is selected and how big it
    is, messages, and the free space of the volume.

Names that begin with a dot are hidden until Show Hidden Files (Ctrl+H) is
on. The window title says where you are. The Explorer looks at the folder
twice a second and shows files that appeared or vanished by themselves.

# SELECTING
A click selects one item, Ctrl+click adds or removes one, Shift+click selects
a range, a drag over empty space draws a rubber band (with Ctrl or Shift it
adds to the selection), Ctrl+A selects everything and Esc selects nothing.
From the keyboard the arrows, Home, End, Page Up and Page Down move (with
Shift they extend the selection, with Ctrl they only move). Typing letters
jumps to the next name that begins with them; Esc or a second of quiet
forgets them.

# COPYING, MOVING AND DELETING
@ Copy, Cut, Paste
    Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X and Ctrl+V. The clipboard is a file in `/tmp`, so you can
    copy in one Explorer window and paste in another. Pasting a copy into the
    same folder makes "name (copy).ext".
@ Drag and drop
    Drag the selection onto a folder or a place. Within one volume the items
    are moved, to another volume they are copied; hold Ctrl to copy or Shift
    to move. Dropping on the Netherworld deletes them.
@ Names that are taken
    The job asks: Keep both ("name (2).ext"), Replace or Skip, with a box to
    use the answer for all the others. Two folders of the same name are
    merged. Moving between volumes copies each item and removes the original
    after its copy succeeded, so a failure leaves the rest where it was.
@ Rename
    F2, or Rename in the context menu, edits the name in place (the extension
    is not selected). New Folder and New File make an item called "New
    Folder" or "New File.txt" and start renaming it.
@ Properties
    Alt+Enter shows size, times, type and volume, and lets you change the
    permission bits; the superuser can also change the owner and the group.

Copies keep permissions and modification times. Links are copied as links;
devices, pipes and sockets are left alone and reported. The root, the
Netherworld itself and mounted volumes cannot be deleted or moved.

# THE NETHERWORLD
`/Netherworld` is the trash, and it lives in memory like the rest of the
root. Delete (and "Send to the Netherworld") moves things there and leaves a
small note, `/Netherworld/.trashinfo/NAME.info`, saying where they came from.
Things from another volume are copied in, except files too big for the memory
that is free. If the name is taken the new arrival becomes NAME.1, NAME.2 and
so on, like the trash(1) command.

Inside the Netherworld a banner offers Restore, which puts the selection back
where it came from (the folders are made again if they are gone, and a name
that is taken asks the usual question), and Empty it. Things placed there by
trash(1) have no note and are restored to your home folder. Delete for Good
(Shift+Delete) and Empty ask first: Yuyuko eats what she is given and cannot
give it back.

# OPENING FILES
Enter or a double click opens the item. A folder is entered. Text files (by
extension or by looking inside) open in the Notebook, `.ppm`, `.pgm`, `.pbm`
and `.bmp` pictures in the Sketchbook, and a program (a file with an execute
bit) is run in a terminal window that waits for Enter when it has finished.
The context menu offers each of these by hand. Anything else asks whether to
open it as text.

# KEYS
@ Enter, Backspace, Alt+Left, Alt+Right, Alt+Up, Alt+Home
    Open, up one folder, back, forward, up, home.
@ F5, Ctrl+L or F6, Ctrl+H, Ctrl+1, Ctrl+2
    Read again, address field, hidden files, list, icons.
@ Delete, Shift+Delete, F2, Alt+Enter
    Send to the Netherworld, delete for good, rename, properties.
@ Ctrl+N, Ctrl+Shift+N, Ctrl+Shift+F, Ctrl+W, F1
    New window, new folder, new file, close, the list of keys.

# FILES
@ ~/.config/explorer.conf
    The view (list or icons), the sort column and order, and whether hidden
    files are shown; kept between runs.
@ /tmp/.explorer-clip-UID
    The clipboard of the Explorer for the user with that number.
@ /Netherworld, /Netherworld/.trashinfo
    The trash and its notes.

# ENVIRONMENT
@ EXPLORER_TRACE
    If set, progress lines starting with `ex:` are printed on standard output.
    The tests read them.

# EXIT STATUS
0 normally, 1 if there is no window server. `explorer --selftest` prints the
number of checks that passed and exits with 1 if any failed.

# NOTES
The Explorer needs the window server (`fairyd`); the self test does not.

# SEE ALSO
trash(1), cp(1), mv(1), rm(1), chmod(1), df(1), gensokyo(7)
